00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:43.570
First off, let's talk terminology.

00:02:43.570 --> 00:02:46.650
In the past, monogamy
used to mean one marriage

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:49.690
till death do us part but
we currently understand it

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:52.900
to mean engaging in one
relationship at a time.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:55.230
Polyamorous individuals in contrast,

00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:57.690
engage in multiple
relationships at a time,

00:02:57.690 --> 00:03:01.010
or in the case of polygamy
are married to multiple people

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:01.870
at the same time.

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:03.100
Now, it turns out

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:06.540
that despite most of Western
society practicing monogamy,

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:10.140
lots of contemporary philosophers
actually dig polyamory.

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:11.360
And this is nothing new.

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:13.980
Two of the greatest
philosophers of the 20th century

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:16.120
and total power couple, Simone De Beauvoir

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:17.880
and Jean-Paul Sartre were making the case

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:21.000
for consensual polyamory
almost a century ago.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:24.250
De Beauvoir was a pioneer
of second wave feminism

00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:25.870
and Sartre is often credited

00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:28.810
as the father of 20th
century existentialism.

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:30.680
They were basically the Beyonce

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:32.450
and Jay-Z of French philosophy.

00:03:32.450 --> 00:03:35.560
Simone and Jean-Paul met in Paris in 1929

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:36.780
when they were both studying

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:39.380
for an extremely prestigious exam.

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:40.930
It was your classic love story.

00:03:40.930 --> 00:03:42.900
He placed first, she placed second.

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:45.630
Thus academic success lit the flames

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:48.920
of what might be called a
50 year soul partnership

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:52.840
that was sexy and romantic
but not exclusive.

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:56.010
Notably, the two never once
lived under the same roof.

00:03:56.010 --> 00:03:57.810
And though their unconventional love life

00:03:57.810 --> 00:03:59.830
wasn't awesome for their reputations.

00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:00.990
These two were down for it

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:03.680
because it aligned with
their philosophical ideals.

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:06.890
Importantly, De Beauvoir
believed in authentic love

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:07.840
which she described

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:10.820
as the reciprocal
recognition of two freedoms.

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:11.653
In layman's terms,

00:04:11.653 --> 00:04:13.900
neither partner is
subordinate to the other

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:16.460
and neither derives all
their meaning from the other.

00:04:16.460 --> 00:04:17.950
In layman's layman's terms,

00:04:17.950 --> 00:04:19.880
Romeo and Juliet ain't (beep).

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:21.330
Sartre took it a step further,

00:04:21.330 --> 00:04:24.510
love wasn't a blissful
merging of two freedoms

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:26.450
but a constant struggle.

00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:29.750
In a monogamous relationship,
you dominate or are dominated.

00:04:29.750 --> 00:04:32.210
Either way, freedom is lost.

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:34.660
For him, the best way to handle this issue

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:37.760
was to leave himself and
his partner open to loving

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:39.770
as many people as they wanted.

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:40.630
And interestingly,

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:43.070
their respective positions
parallel elements

00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:45.800
of the philosophies they were
espousing at the time, too.

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:48.030
So were these brainiacs onto something

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:50.880
or were they just two
fun French alcoholics?

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:53.840
To understand why Sartre and
De Beauvoir's relationship

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:56.410
still feels weird or unusual,

00:04:56.410 --> 00:04:59.000
we need to look at how
monogamy became the norm.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:01.080
Monogamy as we know it
has only been a thing

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:04.100
since the late 17th to early 18th century.

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:06.300
At the time, philosophers
like Immanuel Kant

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:08.590
and Friedrich Nietzsche
were totally on board.

00:05:08.590 --> 00:05:11.580
Kant saw marriage as the
only solution to the problem

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:14.210
of using people for
purely selfish reasons.

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:16.840
By being married, you use your partner.

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:19.550
They use you back and the cycle goes on.

00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:24.150
So a little less selfish and
super romantic, am I right?

00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:26.870
Then again, he also thought
the marital sex cycle

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:30.040
was only virtuous if
procreation was a possibility.

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:32.440
So if you have really loved boning down,

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:34.840
get ready for a cheaper
by the dozen scenario.

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:37.920
Nietzsche was even less
lovey-dovey about relationships.

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:41.230
He was pro-monogamy, anti-romance.

00:05:41.230 --> 00:05:43.530
His advice was to marry your best friend

00:05:43.530 --> 00:05:45.700
which is a very sweet start

00:05:45.700 --> 00:05:47.740
but he also advised ignoring appearances

00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:49.870
and romantic feelings completely.

00:05:49.870 --> 00:05:53.400
He also had some pretty
unsavory views surrounding love.

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:57.030
Like that all your wife
needs to be happy is a baby.

00:05:57.030 --> 00:05:58.290
So it's no surprise

00:05:58.290 --> 00:06:01.040
several of his marriage
proposals were turned down

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:03.380
and he died lonely and miserable.

00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:06.370
But if monogamy is still
a relatively new concept,

00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:08.800
what were we doing before, you ask?

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:09.980
Well, in ancient times,

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:12.060
the goal of marriage was procreation.

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:14.170
So polygamy was A-okay.

00:06:14.170 --> 00:06:15.490
We should note that historically,

00:06:15.490 --> 00:06:17.420
the majority of polygamous situations

00:06:17.420 --> 00:06:20.090
came in the one man to
multiple women model.

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:22.210
While this sounds not
ideal for the ladies,

00:06:22.210 --> 00:06:23.490
there were advantages.

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:25.000
When survival was at stake,

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:26.570
you were much better off as the wife

00:06:26.570 --> 00:06:28.360
of a resource rich fat cat

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:31.340
than as the Miss Weasley
of a penny-less beggar.

00:06:31.340 --> 00:06:33.920
And we know society swung this way

00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:36.290
because of Hammurabi's Code and the Bible,

00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:38.690
which agree that if your
wife couldn't reproduce,

00:06:38.690 --> 00:06:42.160
there's no shame or sin
in taking one who can.

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:44.450
Then Greece and Rome changed everything

00:06:44.450 --> 00:06:47.010
with the creation of
socially imposed monogamy,

00:06:47.010 --> 00:06:48.550
which at the time was geared more

00:06:48.550 --> 00:06:50.550
toward practicality than romance.

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:53.080
You see, monogamy is far better suited

00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:54.620
to building larger armies.

00:06:54.620 --> 00:06:56.440
That's because polygamous societies ran

00:06:56.440 --> 00:06:58.830
into female scarcity problems.

00:06:58.830 --> 00:07:00.000
With multiple women married off

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.090
to the same high ranking group of men,

00:07:02.090 --> 00:07:05.050
the common man was kind of screwed

00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:08.202
or not screwed, as it were yet.

00:07:08.202 --> 00:07:09.035
You get it?

00:07:09.035 --> 00:07:10.050
'Cause he doesn't get to (beep) anyone.

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:12.790
So he usually either skipped
town in search of a wife

00:07:12.790 --> 00:07:14.810
or turned to crime.

00:07:14.810 --> 00:07:16.820
When society shifted toward monogamy,

00:07:16.820 --> 00:07:18.720
there were finally enough women

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:20.350
to go around for all the men,

00:07:20.350 --> 00:07:22.060
regardless of their social status.

00:07:22.060 --> 00:07:24.930
With wives on lock, men would
more productively contribute

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:28.290
to society by doing stuff
like inventing the aqueducts

00:07:28.290 --> 00:07:32.370
and going to war and thus
monogamous marriage became law.

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:35.150
Although you could have
sex with other people.

00:07:35.150 --> 00:07:36.710
So don't worry about that.

00:07:36.710 --> 00:07:39.560
But despite Greece's shift
towards monogamous marriage,

00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:41.370
Plato was pro-poly.

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:44.060
In his perfect world,
families would be communal.

00:07:44.060 --> 00:07:46.100
People would take on lovers on mass

00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:48.860
and sickly offspring would
be thrown off a cliff.

00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:51.980
Plato also came up with the
concept of platonic love,

00:07:51.980 --> 00:07:54.020
which has been
misinterpreted over the years

00:07:54.020 --> 00:07:55.730
like a bad game of telephone.

00:07:55.730 --> 00:07:57.830
Whereas people today see platonic love

00:07:57.830 --> 00:07:59.360
as sexless friendship.

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:02.050
Plato originally
described a ladder of love

00:08:02.050 --> 00:08:04.370
where the bottom rung is carnal attraction

00:08:04.370 --> 00:08:05.820
to someone's physical appearance.

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:08.020
And the top rung is platonic love

00:08:08.020 --> 00:08:10.890
in which one is more
entranced by someone's mind

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:14.010
than their body and contemplates
more spiritual matters

00:08:14.010 --> 00:08:15.810
like supreme beauty and the divine.

00:08:15.810 --> 00:08:17.890
But as Christianity rose to dominance,

00:08:17.890 --> 00:08:20.900
it did not subscribe to
Plato's pro-poly messaging

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:22.970
with a side of chucking
kids off mountains.

00:08:22.970 --> 00:08:24.610
Instead, in the Middle Ages

00:08:24.610 --> 00:08:27.370
the Christian Church
doubled down on monogamy.

00:08:27.370 --> 00:08:30.430
See, Christianity added religious
stakes to the whole thing.

00:08:30.430 --> 00:08:32.010
If you wanted to make God happy,

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:34.940
you would share your body
with only one other person.

00:08:34.940 --> 00:08:37.550
Nevermind that the Bible
has plenty of polygamists

00:08:37.550 --> 00:08:38.800
like Jacob and Solomon.

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:39.800
As is often the case,

00:08:39.800 --> 00:08:41.400
there was an economic motivation

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:43.290
to push the idea of monogamy.

00:08:43.290 --> 00:08:44.290
It was the best way

00:08:44.290 --> 00:08:47.230
for the church to get
the aristocracy's riches.

00:08:47.230 --> 00:08:49.740
After all, one spouse
means fewer opportunities

00:08:49.740 --> 00:08:50.610
to have children,

00:08:50.610 --> 00:08:53.240
especially sons who could
inherit your wealth.

00:08:53.240 --> 00:08:54.720
Don't have a son when you die?

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:56.640
Oh no, guess all your money is going

00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:58.220
to your younger brother instead,

00:08:58.220 --> 00:09:00.270
who, while you were busy being a Lord,

00:09:00.270 --> 00:09:02.310
very likely has joined the clergy.

00:09:02.310 --> 00:09:05.950
It was a pretty plush and
popular gig for second sons.

00:09:05.950 --> 00:09:07.880
Your money becomes his money

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:10.090
which becomes the church's money.

00:09:10.090 --> 00:09:12.510
So on some level, monogamy shifted

00:09:12.510 --> 00:09:15.660
from being a mating system,
centered around procreation

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:17.130
to being a marriage system

00:09:17.130 --> 00:09:19.600
that ensured the church's
coffers stayed full.

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:20.433
This continued on

00:09:20.433 --> 00:09:23.030
until around the time of
the French Revolution.

00:09:23.030 --> 00:09:24.470
By this point, people were tired

00:09:24.470 --> 00:09:27.280
of marriages of reason,
an enlightenment era trend

00:09:27.280 --> 00:09:29.060
where people were
encouraged to get married

00:09:29.060 --> 00:09:30.870
purely out of financial interest

00:09:30.870 --> 00:09:34.650
because it turns out these led
to very unfavorable outcomes,

00:09:34.650 --> 00:09:36.900
like adultery or domestic violence.

00:09:36.900 --> 00:09:38.970
So we kicked reason to the curb,

00:09:38.970 --> 00:09:41.960
transitioned from the age of
enlightenment to romanticism

00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:44.610
and embraced marriages of feeling.

00:09:44.610 --> 00:09:46.423
Release the doves.

00:09:48.820 --> 00:09:50.380
Okay who the (beep) forgot the doves?.

00:09:50.380 --> 00:09:52.300
Like angsty teens determined

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:53.700
to be the opposite of their parents,

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:56.190
romantics hated unions predicated

00:09:56.190 --> 00:09:58.040
on finances and practicalities.

00:09:58.040 --> 00:09:59.710
Sure, some people were still trying

00:09:59.710 --> 00:10:01.440
to land the richest dude in town

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:03.590
but the overall vibes had changed.

00:10:03.590 --> 00:10:07.040
Because romantics were adorably
hopeful about marriage.

00:10:07.040 --> 00:10:09.660
They believed that the honeymoon
phase should last forever

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:12.910
and dopamine-induced
passion should never fade.

00:10:12.910 --> 00:10:14.730
- You guys are so sweet together.

00:10:14.730 --> 00:10:16.230
- Because we're still on our honeymoon.

00:10:16.230 --> 00:10:19.158
- 35 wonderful years of marriage.

00:10:19.158 --> 00:10:19.991
- Feels like two to me.

00:10:19.991 --> 00:10:21.360
- Note scientists have now determined

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:24.210
that dopamine release as a
result of relationship excitement

00:10:24.210 --> 00:10:27.320
fades over time and eventually stops.

00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:28.153
Yikes.

00:10:28.153 --> 00:10:30.610
But back then, this natural
occurrence was a sign

00:10:30.610 --> 00:10:32.610
that your marriage was on the rocks.

00:10:32.610 --> 00:10:35.900
Romantics also believed that
true love means accepting

00:10:35.900 --> 00:10:38.680
and delighting in everything
about your partner,

00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:41.110
including the fact that her
family visits her constantly.

00:10:41.110 --> 00:10:42.473
And they're always there.

00:10:43.350 --> 00:10:45.190
You never get to be alone,
they're always there.

00:10:45.190 --> 00:10:47.040
The madness didn't stop there.

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:48.900
In an incredibly destructive move,

00:10:48.900 --> 00:10:51.830
they made love synonymous with sex.

00:10:51.830 --> 00:10:53.810
Mind-blowing frequent sex

00:10:53.810 --> 00:10:56.010
became the benchmark
of a good relationship

00:10:56.010 --> 00:10:58.500
and infrequent sex and or infidelity

00:10:58.500 --> 00:11:00.570
suddenly became huge offenses.

00:11:00.570 --> 00:11:03.600
True love became a
cure-all for loneliness.

00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:04.930
Why complete yourself

00:11:04.930 --> 00:11:07.860
when your partner can
Jerry McGuire it for you?

00:11:07.860 --> 00:11:12.230
- You complete me.

00:11:12.230 --> 00:11:13.770
- Romanticism also taught us

00:11:13.770 --> 00:11:14.603
that we should be able

00:11:14.603 --> 00:11:17.340
to intuit our partner's
thoughts and needs.

00:11:17.340 --> 00:11:21.090
That's right, romanticism
discouraged open communication.

00:11:21.090 --> 00:11:24.460
Society so wholly embraced
these romantic ideals

00:11:24.460 --> 00:11:25.930
that even in the secular world,

00:11:25.930 --> 00:11:27.700
it was to your political advantage

00:11:27.700 --> 00:11:30.970
to make sure everyone knew
how much you loved your wife.

00:11:30.970 --> 00:11:34.800
Is this sounding eerily familiar
and certifiably chaotic?

00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:36.210
It should, because the template

00:11:36.210 --> 00:11:38.100
for contemporary romance was laid

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:39.850
by these moon-eyed romantics

00:11:39.850 --> 00:11:42.250
and their unrealistic
understanding of love

00:11:42.250 --> 00:11:44.920
is a major reason why we
suffer from heartbreak

00:11:44.920 --> 00:11:48.070
and spend a crap ton on
couple's therapy today.

00:11:48.070 --> 00:11:51.200
So why are we still doing
this chaotic tap dance

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:53.360
from over 200 years ago?

00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:56.270
In part, you can thank
the industrial revolution.

00:11:56.270 --> 00:11:57.980
Which brought an influx of people

00:11:57.980 --> 00:11:59.460
moving from farms to cities.

00:11:59.460 --> 00:12:01.530
As a result, people became less concerned

00:12:01.530 --> 00:12:03.570
with having enough kids to man a plow

00:12:03.570 --> 00:12:04.830
and with fewer kids to wrangle

00:12:04.830 --> 00:12:07.840
it became easier to focus on
the quality of your marriage.

00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:10.040
And the weakening of
religious and community bonds

00:12:10.040 --> 00:12:12.230
made monogamy more important than ever.

00:12:12.230 --> 00:12:13.750
Fewer friends and family members meant

00:12:13.750 --> 00:12:16.010
you needed your partner
to take on more roles.

00:12:16.010 --> 00:12:18.810
Best friend, therapist, sex God

00:12:18.810 --> 00:12:20.760
and hey, monogamy has its perks.

00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:22.310
Committing to one partner over time

00:12:22.310 --> 00:12:23.770
can lead to greater intimacy

00:12:23.770 --> 00:12:26.730
and it can feel nice to have
someone's undivided attention.

00:12:26.730 --> 00:12:27.670
Interestingly enough,

00:12:27.670 --> 00:12:30.430
polyamory and swinging did
have a brief resurgence

00:12:30.430 --> 00:12:33.490
after World War II
created a scarcity of men.

00:12:33.490 --> 00:12:35.750
In a less dramatic version
of "Y: The Last Man",

00:12:35.750 --> 00:12:39.080
widows of the war were taken
in by married men who survived.

00:12:39.080 --> 00:12:39.940
Group marriage

00:12:39.940 --> 00:12:43.010
and open relationships
ramped up in the 60s and 70s

00:12:43.010 --> 00:12:45.000
until AIDS took all the momentum

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:46.260
out of the free love movement.

00:12:46.260 --> 00:12:48.940
But nobody puts polyamory in a corner.

00:12:48.940 --> 00:12:51.270
Eventually the internet
gave us broader access

00:12:51.270 --> 00:12:52.530
to potential partners

00:12:52.530 --> 00:12:54.770
and apps helped poly
people find each other.

00:12:54.770 --> 00:12:56.360
But we're not finished talking

00:12:56.360 --> 00:12:59.150
about the connection between
wealth and monogamous marriage.

00:12:59.150 --> 00:13:02.210
Socially imposed monogamy,
even in loving marriages,

00:13:02.210 --> 00:13:04.690
still functions as a
way to protect wealth.

00:13:04.690 --> 00:13:06.560
And couples who got
married in the 20th century

00:13:06.560 --> 00:13:08.270
were better able to pull resources

00:13:08.270 --> 00:13:10.140
since women were more
likely to have access

00:13:10.140 --> 00:13:12.150
to their own money or an inheritance.

00:13:12.150 --> 00:13:13.780
And today not only are rich

00:13:13.780 --> 00:13:16.020
and well-educated people
more likely to marry.

00:13:16.020 --> 00:13:18.130
They're also less likely to get divorced.

00:13:18.130 --> 00:13:19.680
And because people typically marry

00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:22.850
within their socioeconomic
class, the rich get richer

00:13:22.850 --> 00:13:25.420
while the poor remain unhitched and broke.

00:13:25.420 --> 00:13:27.890
Fewer people are walking down
the aisle in the first place

00:13:27.890 --> 00:13:30.330
because millennials are
putting financial security

00:13:30.330 --> 00:13:32.870
before marriage, cohabitation
is more widely accepted

00:13:32.870 --> 00:13:35.300
and weddings are (beep) expensive.

00:13:35.300 --> 00:13:37.110
At this point, marriage could be seen

00:13:37.110 --> 00:13:39.110
as more of an achievement of status

00:13:39.110 --> 00:13:40.760
rather than something everyone does

00:13:40.760 --> 00:13:42.530
regardless of where they are in life.

00:13:42.530 --> 00:13:44.660
Of course, just because
you're rich and married

00:13:44.660 --> 00:13:47.160
doesn't necessarily
mean you are monogamous.

00:13:47.160 --> 00:13:49.770
A study by Match.com, of all places,

00:13:49.770 --> 00:13:51.710
found that polyamorous relationships

00:13:51.710 --> 00:13:53.180
aren't just for rich influencers

00:13:53.180 --> 00:13:55.150
like Bella Thorne or Jake Paul.

00:13:55.150 --> 00:13:56.620
There's a willingness to engage

00:13:56.620 --> 00:13:59.540
in polyamorous relationships,
independent of race,

00:13:59.540 --> 00:14:01.470
gender and socioeconomic status.

00:14:01.470 --> 00:14:03.220
But the question continues to puzzle

00:14:03.220 --> 00:14:04.770
many (mumbles) enthusiasts.

00:14:04.770 --> 00:14:06.690
What relationship formation are humans

00:14:06.690 --> 00:14:08.090
actually wired for?

00:14:08.090 --> 00:14:11.630
Monogamy is found only in
three to 5% of animal species

00:14:11.630 --> 00:14:13.730
but polyamory does have its challenges

00:14:13.730 --> 00:14:16.050
for humans from an
evolutionary perspective.

00:14:16.050 --> 00:14:18.240
If fathers wanted to
protect their offspring

00:14:18.240 --> 00:14:20.060
from being clubbed by other males,

00:14:20.060 --> 00:14:21.700
they couldn't just Don Draper their way

00:14:21.700 --> 00:14:23.310
through the paleolithic period.

00:14:23.310 --> 00:14:24.900
Instead, they had to stick around

00:14:24.900 --> 00:14:26.860
and offer bi-parental care.

00:14:26.860 --> 00:14:28.830
Of course, this came with its own reward.

00:14:28.830 --> 00:14:30.050
They could be all but certain

00:14:30.050 --> 00:14:32.070
that their kid wasn't the milkman's.

00:14:32.070 --> 00:14:33.140
Though in caveman times,

00:14:33.140 --> 00:14:35.180
that was the guy who milked mammoths.

00:14:35.180 --> 00:14:37.710
Other fun theories suggest
polyamorous societies

00:14:37.710 --> 00:14:39.620
could have been wiped out by STDs.

00:14:39.620 --> 00:14:40.670
The jury's still out

00:14:40.670 --> 00:14:43.540
on whether we're innately
monogamous or polyamorous

00:14:43.540 --> 00:14:45.260
but most scientists have concluded

00:14:45.260 --> 00:14:47.020
we're somewhere in between.

00:14:47.020 --> 00:14:49.190
In other words, monogamish.

00:14:49.190 --> 00:14:53.180
But evolutionary wiring aside,
what's actually best for us

00:14:53.180 --> 00:14:56.410
as intelligent and enlightened
humans of the 21st century?

00:14:56.410 --> 00:14:58.410
Some contemporary philosophers would argue

00:14:58.410 --> 00:15:01.530
that monogamy is more practical
from a resources standpoint.

00:15:01.530 --> 00:15:03.240
Who has the time and spaghetti budget

00:15:03.240 --> 00:15:05.520
to Lady and the Tramp multiple partners.

00:15:05.520 --> 00:15:07.910
There's also the problem of jealousy.

00:15:07.910 --> 00:15:10.530
I mean, romcoms and ideas
like true love's kiss

00:15:10.530 --> 00:15:13.480
are so deeply entrenched in
our psyches from childhood

00:15:13.480 --> 00:15:15.720
that we get a little surge of oxytocin,

00:15:15.720 --> 00:15:17.820
anytime we picture our boo

00:15:17.820 --> 00:15:19.870
taking some other hottie
to the bowling alley.

00:15:19.870 --> 00:15:22.720
Our brains literally
don't want us to share.

00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:24.970
But if that's true,
then why do studies show

00:15:24.970 --> 00:15:26.500
that polyamorous relationships

00:15:26.500 --> 00:15:29.800
actually experience less
jealousy than monogamous ones?

00:15:29.800 --> 00:15:32.090
Well, in successful poly relationships,

00:15:32.090 --> 00:15:34.040
the problem of jealousy is addressed

00:15:34.040 --> 00:15:35.720
through the idea of compersion.

00:15:35.720 --> 00:15:37.460
Which is a relatively new term

00:15:37.460 --> 00:15:40.580
for the whole-hearted
participation in the joy of others.

00:15:40.580 --> 00:15:42.480
It's basically the opposite of jealousy

00:15:42.480 --> 00:15:44.910
where you give your
partner a sincere high five

00:15:44.910 --> 00:15:47.170
after they slept with
Channing Tatum without you.

00:15:47.170 --> 00:15:49.430
Of course, for folks who
aren't into compersion,

00:15:49.430 --> 00:15:51.540
engaging in a polyamorous relationship

00:15:51.540 --> 00:15:53.880
may simply be more stress than it's worth.

00:15:53.880 --> 00:15:56.610
However, contemporary
scholars like Harry Chalmers

00:15:56.610 --> 00:15:59.480
and Carrie Jenkins lean
in the opposite direction.

00:15:59.480 --> 00:16:00.580
And some go so far

00:16:00.580 --> 00:16:03.890
as to say that monogamy isn't
even morally permissible.

00:16:03.890 --> 00:16:05.900
Chalmers argues that putting the brunt

00:16:05.900 --> 00:16:07.570
of our emotional and sexual needs

00:16:07.570 --> 00:16:11.010
on one romantic partner is
unfair and unreasonable.

00:16:11.010 --> 00:16:13.880
Especially when multiple
partners could carry the load.

00:16:13.880 --> 00:16:17.100
Why make one person do
all that emotional labor?

00:16:17.100 --> 00:16:19.470
As for jealousy, these
philosophers would argue

00:16:19.470 --> 00:16:21.270
that we don't restrict friendships.

00:16:21.270 --> 00:16:23.020
So why restrict lovers?

00:16:23.020 --> 00:16:26.100
Romantic love, like friendship
is an important human good.

00:16:26.100 --> 00:16:28.740
Shouldn't we be happy for
our partner and ourselves

00:16:28.740 --> 00:16:30.640
to have more access to Channing Tatum?.

00:16:30.640 --> 00:16:32.220
In the end, it's weird.

00:16:32.220 --> 00:16:33.500
We're all mostly locked

00:16:33.500 --> 00:16:36.420
into the monogamy framework
by centuries of reinforcement.

00:16:36.420 --> 00:16:37.253
But of course,

00:16:37.253 --> 00:16:40.230
it's up for every person to
figure out for themselves.

00:16:40.230 --> 00:16:42.210
Monogamy may be a social construct

00:16:42.210 --> 00:16:44.360
but so is say, using a toilet.

00:16:44.360 --> 00:16:47.010
As University of Washington
professor of psychology,

00:16:47.010 --> 00:16:48.467
David P. Barash puts it,

00:16:48.467 --> 00:16:50.490
"Perhaps what makes human beings special

00:16:50.490 --> 00:16:53.060
is our ability to do
things that are unnatural.

00:16:53.060 --> 00:16:54.550
Whether those things are obsolete

00:16:54.550 --> 00:16:56.930
or like monogamy, are socially imposed

00:16:56.930 --> 00:16:59.330
and thus new to our
evolutionary experience."

00:16:59.330 --> 00:17:00.510
But what do you guys think?

00:17:00.510 --> 00:17:02.860
Should we override our
romantic conditioning

00:17:02.860 --> 00:17:05.310
to experiment with new
types of relationships?

00:17:05.310 --> 00:17:08.170
Is there a future where
polyamory is the norm

00:17:08.170 --> 00:17:09.290
rather than the exception?

00:17:09.290 --> 00:17:11.110
Let us know what you
think in the comments.

00:17:11.110 --> 00:17:13.050
Big thanks to our patreons
for all your support

00:17:13.050 --> 00:17:15.080
and be sure to check out our podcast.

00:17:15.080 --> 00:17:16.250
Hit that subscribe button

00:17:16.250 --> 00:17:18.780
like you're slapping some
sense into the romantic era.

00:17:18.780 --> 00:17:20.710
And don't forget to ring that bell.

00:17:20.710 --> 00:17:22.190
As always, thanks for watching.

00:17:22.190 --> 00:17:23.923
And I love you,

00:17:25.290 --> 00:17:27.420
platonically, but I still love you.

00:17:27.420 --> 00:17:28.551
See you later.

00:17:28.551 --> 00:17:31.134
(upbeat music)

